    "lawCode": "Amd §§25.24, 25.07, 25.23 & 25.29, add §25.06, Arts & Cul L; amd §4, Chap 704 of 1991; amd §11, Chap 151 of 2010",
    "lawSection": "Arts and Cultural Affairs Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9461\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the arts and cultural affairs law, in relation to insti-\ntuting civil penalties for utilizing ticket purchasing software (Part\nA); to amend the arts and cultural affairs law, in relation to requiring\nticket prices to be disclosed prior to a customer purchasing such ticket\nand requiring such price to remain the same during the purchase process\n(Part B); to amend the arts and cultural affairs law, in relation to\nprohibiting the resale of a ticket if such ticket was initially offered\nto the public at no charge (Part C); to amend the arts and cultural\naffairs law, in relation to requiring online resale marketplaces to\nclearly and conspicuously disclose the established price charged by the\noperator of a place of entertainment that is printed or endorsed on the\nface of each ticket (Part D); to amend the arts and cultural affairs\nlaw, in relation to allowing reasonable and actual costs to be charged\nfor the physical delivery of certain tickets (Part E); and to amend\nchapter 704 of the laws of 1991, amending the arts and cultural affairs\nlaw and chapter 912 of the laws of 1920 relating to regulation of boxing\nand wrestling matches relating to tickets to places of entertainment,\nand chapter 151 of the laws of 2010, amending the arts and cultural\naffairs law relating to resale of tickets of places of entertainment, in\nrelation to extending the effectiveness thereof (Part F)\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nPart A of this bill amends paragraph(a)of subdivision 3, and subdivi-\nsions 4, 5 and 6 of section 25.24 of the Arts and Cultural Affairs Law\nto increase civil fines for the use of ticket purchasing software know\nas bots. This part takes effect 60 days after the effective date of the\nact.\n\nPart B of this bill amends subdivision 4 of section 25.07 of the Arts\nand Cultural Affairs Law to require sellers and resellers of tickets to\ndisclose the total cost of a ticket and clearly and conspicuously\ndisclose the amount of the price that is made up of fees and other\ncharges. The disclosure of the total price and fees shall be displayed\nin the ticket listing prior to the ticket being selected for purchase.\nThe price of a ticket may not increase during the purchasing process.\nThe total price of a ticket must be disclosed at the beginning of a\ntransaction at the first instance a price is shown. Such disclosure\ncan't be misleading, the components of the total price may not be\ndisplayed more prominently or in a larger font than the total cost. This\npart takes effect 60 days after the effective date of the act.\n\nPart C of the bill amends the arts and cultural affairs law by adding a\nnew section 25.06 that bans the resale of tickets originally provided to\nthe public free and access to a free ticket was not contingent on\npurchasing something else. This part takes effect 60 days after the\neffective date of the act.\n\nPart D of the bill amends subdivision 2 of section 25.23 of the arts and\ncultural affairs law to require secondary sellers to disclose the print-\ned or endorsed price of a ticket. This part takes effect 60 days after\nthe effective date of the act.\n\n\nPart E of the bill amends section 25.29 of the arts and cultural affairs\nlaw, prohibiting the charging of a delivery fee for tickets delivered\nelectronically or for self-printing by the buyer. This part takes effect\n60 days after the effective date of the act.\n\nPart F of the bill extends the current provisions of Article 25 of the\nArts and Cultural Affairs Law out to June 30, 2025. This part take\neffect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nArticle 25 of the New York State Arts & Cultural Affairs Law has been\nhistorically subject to constant renewal and evolution by the Legisla-\nture. This bill once again extends and updates New York's \"Ticketing\nLaw\" by incorporating policy recommendations from a 2020-21 legislative\ninvestigation into the state's live event ticket industry due to\nconcerns about potentially unfair, deceptive, and anti-consumer prac-\ntices occurring in the primary and secondary markets.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately; provided, however, that the\napplicable effective date of Parts A through F of this act shall be as\nspecifically set forth in the last section of such Parts.\n",
